  "paragraphs": {
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "index": 0,
        "blockIndex": 0,
        "paragraphInBlock": 0,
        "text": "We hear many different sounds every day.\nSinging, taut instrument strings, and whistles can give musical tones.\nExplosions and a running internal-combustion engine can give noise."
      },
      {
        "index": 1,
        "blockIndex": 0,
        "paragraphInBlock": 1,
        "text": "Hissing snakes and barking or howling dogs are further examples.\nOur vocal organs can make musical tones as well as noise.\nThis familiar contrast leads us to examine the vibrations."
      },
      {
        "index": 2,
        "blockIndex": 1,
        "paragraphInBlock": 0,
        "text": "**Musical tones** How do musical tones differ physically from noise?\nWhy do different musical tones sound different?"
      },
      {
        "index": 3,
        "blockIndex": 2,
        "paragraphInBlock": 0,
        "text": "An almost pure tone can be made with a **tuning fork**.\nFigure 81 shows a fork fixed to a wooden box open at the top.\nA light hammer strikes one of its two arms."
      },
      {
        "index": 4,
        "blockIndex": 2,
        "paragraphInBlock": 1,
        "text": "The vibrating arms produce sound waves.\nTheir vibrations gradually die away.\nThe sound becomes quieter as the vibration amplitude falls."
      },
      {
        "index": 5,
        "blockIndex": 2,
        "paragraphInBlock": 2,
        "text": "To record the motion, attach a needle to one arm.\nLet it slide uniformly along a soot-blackened glass plate.\nIts vibration leaves a trace nearly shaped like a sine curve."
      },
      {
        "index": 6,
        "blockIndex": 2,
        "paragraphInBlock": 3,
        "text": "Figure 82 shows this trace.\nUniform sliding spreads the motion out in time along the plate.\nThis is called a **time trace** of the vibration."
      },
      {
        "index": 7,
        "blockIndex": 2,
        "paragraphInBlock": 4,
        "text": "It resembles the pattern made with a sand pendulum.\nThe nearly sinusoidal trace shows nearly harmonic vibration.\nThis is how the tuning fork's motion is identified."
      },
      {
        "index": 8,
        "blockIndex": 7,
        "paragraphInBlock": 0,
        "text": "**Pure tone** Sound from harmonic vibration is called a pure tone.\nThis ideal sound has a single frequency.\nA general musical tone may contain several related frequencies."
      },
      {
        "index": 9,
        "blockIndex": 8,
        "paragraphInBlock": 0,
        "text": "We hear differences in both loudness and pitch.\nLoudness tells us how loud a sound seems, pitch how high it seems."
      },
      {
        "index": 10,
        "blockIndex": 9,
        "paragraphInBlock": 0,
        "text": "**Loudness** is how loud a sound seems to a listener.\nCompare the same fork at the same listening position.\nA harder strike makes the vibration amplitude larger."
      },
      {
        "index": 11,
        "blockIndex": 9,
        "paragraphInBlock": 1,
        "text": "The fork then generally sounds louder.\nThis comparison keeps the frequency and listening conditions the same.\nLoudness also depends on how the ear responds."
      },
      {
        "index": 12,
        "blockIndex": 10,
        "paragraphInBlock": 0,
        "text": "Our ears are not equally sensitive to all frequencies.\nEqual sound-wave amplitudes need not sound equally loud.\nChanging frequency can change the loudness we hear."
      },
      {
        "index": 13,
        "blockIndex": 10,
        "paragraphInBlock": 1,
        "text": "The source places greatest sensitivity near 3500 Hz.\nThis is an approximate description, not a fixed rule.\nSensitivity depends on the listener and sound level."
      },
      {
        "index": 14,
        "blockIndex": 11,
        "paragraphInBlock": 0,
        "text": "**Pitch** is how high or low a tone sounds.\nCompare tuning forks of different sizes.\nAttach a needle to an arm of each sounding fork."
      },
      {
        "index": 15,
        "blockIndex": 11,
        "paragraphInBlock": 1,
        "text": "Record each motion on soot-blackened glass.\nUse the same uniform sliding speed for each record.\nThe higher tone gives a sine-like trace with a shorter period."
      },
      {
        "index": 16,
        "blockIndex": 11,
        "paragraphInBlock": 2,
        "text": "A shorter period means more vibrations in the same time.\nIts vibration frequency is therefore higher.\nFor a pure tone, frequency determines pitch."
      },
      {
        "index": 17,
        "blockIndex": 12,
        "paragraphInBlock": 0,
        "text": "A vibrating string shows the same link to pitch.\nKeep the string and its vibrating length unchanged.\nIncreasing its tension raises its natural frequency."
      },
      {
        "index": 18,
        "blockIndex": 12,
        "paragraphInBlock": 1,
        "text": "A guitar's tuning peg can tighten the string.\nThe tighter string vibrates faster.\nIt therefore produces a higher tone."
      },
      {
        "index": 19,
        "blockIndex": 13,
        "paragraphInBlock": 0,
        "text": "**Noise** Typical noise mixes many frequencies irregularly.\nIt has no single stable pitch.\nMusical tones can also mix frequencies, in a repeating pattern."
      },
      {
        "index": 20,
        "blockIndex": 13,
        "paragraphInBlock": 1,
        "text": "Such a pattern can give a definite pitch.\nSome real sounds combine a tone with noise.\nThe distinction is not simply loud versus quiet."
      }
    ],
